MLX
https://github.com/ml-explore/mlx
Copyright (c) 2023 Apple Inc.

Pinned submodule at `lib/mlx-src`, built by `scripts/build-mlx.sh` and linked
into the sushi binary. Unmodified as a dependency.

Separately, Metal source from MLX was ported into `src/transformer.zig`: the
fused head-dim-256 prefill attention kernel (`sushi_attn_p256`) began as a
self-contained port of MLX's steel attention (FA-2 online softmax, float32
accumulation, exp2 softmax) specialized to head_dim 256, and its fragment
layout mirrors MLX's `BaseMMAFrag<float,8,8>`. Modifications by mlx-serve: the
v2 tiling (BQ=64, BK=32, 8 simdgroups, register-resident Q, uint4 staging) was
picked by our own microbenchmark sweep, and a sliding-window band arm and
key-axis chunked dispatch were added.

The verifier expert-pair kernel (`VERIFY_EXPERT_REUSE_SOURCE`) adapts the
4-bit affine `qmv_impl`, `load_vector` and `qdot` arithmetic from MLX's
`backend/metal/kernels/quantized.h`. Modifications: sorted expert-run pairing,
shared packed-weight reads for two inputs, and verification-only shape guards.
The fused expert reduction preserves the bf16 partial-sum order of MLX
`reduction/reduce_col.h:col_reduce_small` while gathering, weighting and
reducing in one dispatch. The batched coarse-head pair kernel
(`MTP_COARSE_PAIRS_SOURCE`) adapts the 3-bit affine qmv-fast reduction from
quantized.h, sharing each packed read across two input rows.

The row-identical 8-bit projection kernel in `src/mtp_qmv.zig` adapts
`qmv_fast_impl`, `qmv_impl`, `load_vector` and `qdot` from MLX
`backend/metal/kernels/quantized.h` (Copyright (c) 2023-2024 Apple Inc.).
It shares packed weights across input rows while preserving each serial
row's lane assignment, reduction order and output rounding.

The eight-row DFlash FFN kernel and the fused gate/up/SiLU kernel in
`src/dflash_qmv.zig` adapt `qmv_wide_impl` and affine four-bit `dequantize`
from MLX `backend/metal/kernels/quantized.h`. They specialize the A4
group-64 FFN geometries and share decoded weights across the input rows
while retaining MLX's subchunk accumulation and lane-shuffle reduction order.

The HC RMS kernel in `src/glm5_hc_prefill.zig` adapts the four-value-per-thread
`rms_looped` reduction from MLX `backend/metal/kernels/rms_norm.metal`
(Copyright 2024 Apple Inc.), also reflected in oMLX's HC normalization helper.
It reads BF16 residuals directly and retains FP32 normalization before the mix.

The affine QKV kernel in `src/glm5_decode.zig` also adapts `qmv_fast_impl`,
`load_vector` and `qdot` from MLX quantized.h through oMLX's multi_qmv wrapper.
It preserves each independent row's arithmetic while selecting three banks
inside one dispatch; no concatenated weight buffer is created.

The row-identical dense projection kernels in `src/mtp_dense_rows.zig`
adapt MLX v0.32.2 `backend/metal/kernels/gemv.h` and `dot.h`.
Modifications: independent activation rows share a dispatch, with the
serial BF16 router and scalar-gate reduction trees preserved.

Several other kernels of ours deliberately reproduce the exact arithmetic of
MLX ops (reduction order, accumulator dtype, `fast::exp`, the `precise::rsqrt`
variant) so a fused kernel stays bit-identical to the op chain it replaces.
Those are our own implementations written against MLX's observable behaviour,
not copies of its source.

The original QSA NAX kernel adapted MLX's `steel/attn/nax.h` (Copyright
(c) 2025 Apple Inc., MIT License). The dense-cache branches of `src/kernels/qsa_nax.metal`
and `src/kernels/qsa_nax_header.metal` port oMLX's occupancy-tuned QSA
kernel from `omlx/patches/mlx_vlm_qwen4_exp_compat/vendor/mlx_vlm/models/
qwen4_exp/qsa_nax.py` at d6b2b92 (Apache-2.0).
https://github.com/jundot/omlx/pull/4020
Original mlx-serve QSA NAX implementation contributed by Nikolai V.
The packed-cache branches retain the original MLX-derived split-head-dimension
implementation and two-term bf16 PV. Dense-cache port via mlx-serve PR #636
(bfe518f), contributed by Samuel Reed (@STRML).

`src/kernels/attn_pd_nax_header.metal` adapts the same cooperative fragment
layout and MMA wrapper from `steel/attn/nax.h`, and `src/kernels/attn_pd_nax.metal`
follows the structure of MLX's NAX steel attention (Copyright (c) 2025 Apple
Inc., MIT License): 16 query rows per simdgroup, 16x32x16 matmul2d tiles, K/V
fragments read from device memory. Modifications: asymmetric query/key and
value widths, the SIMD kernel's key-axis chunked dispatch with fp32 carries,
cache-coordinate causal and sliding-band masks, per-head sinks, and a two-term
bf16 PV.

